WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.200
Think of an enzyme like a toll booth.

2
00:00:05.620 --> 00:00:10.340
When traffic is light, low substrate, every car gets processed as fast as it

3
00:00:10.340 --> 00:00:13.600
arrives, so velocity is proportional to substrate.

4
00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:17.400
When substrate equals Km, you're at half capacity.

5
00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:22.720
And when traffic is heavy, high substrate, the booth is saturated, working flat out

6
00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:26.420
at Vmax. Adding more cars doesn't make the worker any faster.

7
00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:29.240
Saturation doesn't mean the enzyme stops.

8
00:00:29.780 --> 00:00:32.560
It means it's already going as fast as it possibly can.

9
00:00:33.333 --> 00:00:36.493
This curve is the Rosetta Stone of enzyme kinetics.

10
00:00:37.053 --> 00:00:39.733
Almost every idea you'll meet maps onto it.

11
00:00:40.093 --> 00:00:45.373
At low substrate concentration, the rate rises nearly linearly, and the enzyme's

12
00:00:45.373 --> 00:00:48.173
performance depends on kcat over Km.

13
00:00:48.673 --> 00:00:50.933
Push higher, and you hit diminishing returns.

14
00:00:51.533 --> 00:00:54.013
At high substrate, the enzyme is saturated.

15
00:00:54.633 --> 00:00:59.793
Adding more barely changes the rate, and you're at Vmax, which equals kcat times

16
00:00:59.793 --> 00:01:01.133
the enzyme concentration.

17
00:01:01.793 --> 00:01:06.773
Km sits at the midpoint of the curve, where velocity is half of Vmax.

18
00:01:07.373 --> 00:01:11.613
Read it as how much substrate the enzyme needs to get going, not automatically as a

19
00:01:11.613 --> 00:01:12.613
measure of affinity.

20
00:01:13.267 --> 00:01:15.547
Here's what makes Km so useful.

21
00:01:16.067 --> 00:01:18.787
An enzyme can prefer one substrate over another.

22
00:01:19.247 --> 00:01:24.087
The one with the lowest Km is captured most efficiently, reaching half-maximal

23
00:01:24.087 --> 00:01:25.767
speed at the lowest concentration.

24
00:01:26.467 --> 00:01:29.947
It's like a vending machine that takes quarters, dimes, and pennies.

25
00:01:30.567 --> 00:01:34.387
Same output, but it handles quarters most smoothly.

26
00:01:34.987 --> 00:01:40.507
And remarkably, an enzyme's Km is usually close to the substrate concentration it

27
00:01:40.507 --> 00:01:42.867
actually sees inside the living cell.

28
00:01:43.667 --> 00:01:46.767
Vmax depends on how much enzyme you added.

29
00:01:47.167 --> 00:01:52.127
So to describe the enzyme molecule itself, we use kcat, the turnover number.

30
00:01:52.587 --> 00:01:57.347
It's the number of reactions one enzyme molecule can run per second when it's fully

31
00:01:57.347 --> 00:01:59.267
saturated. It's top speed.

32
00:01:59.927 --> 00:02:03.287
Carbonic anhydrase clocks an astonishing 600,000 per second.

33
00:02:03.867 --> 00:02:06.987
Lysozyme plods along at about one reaction every two seconds.

34
00:02:07.367 --> 00:02:09.327
But kcat is only top speed.

35
00:02:09.787 --> 00:02:12.647
It doesn't tell you how the enzyme performs when substrate is scarce.

36
00:02:12.647 --> 00:02:16.547
For that, you want kcat over Km, the catalytic efficiency.
